    "label": "Product Manager",
    "scope": "Deciding what a digital or technology product does and why, and steering the team that builds it: owning the product vision and roadmap, understanding user and market needs, setting priorities and owning the backlog the build team works from, and answering for what the product achieves through its measures of success. The product is software, an app, a platform, or a data or AI product built by an engineering team; the employer's industry is irrelevant. Covers every seniority from associate to head of product, and both names the market uses for this one job: product owner and product manager, including their technical, digital, platform and data variants. The edge: managing a financial, insurance or consumer product commercially (rates, fees, coverage, pricing, campaigns) with no team building it from a backlog the person owns is outside, and a product manager title that names a financial or insurance product line (mortgages, cards, loans, deposits, policies) rather than a digital product is that commercial job; so are product marketing, running the delivery (IT Project Manager), and specifying requirements without owning the priorities (Business Analyst).",
